1
00:00:05,375 --> 00:00:09,265
Last time, Basil touched a mimosa, and its leaves folded up.

2
00:00:09,495 --> 00:00:10,705
It was not shy.

3
00:00:10,935 --> 00:00:12,705
It was responding to a change.

4
00:00:12,935 --> 00:00:15,035
Now we are on a farm in Kranji.

5
00:00:19,443 --> 00:00:20,623
Lock in first.

6
00:00:20,843 --> 00:00:22,263
And find the new lizard!

7
00:00:23,839 --> 00:00:24,849
Five eggs.

8
00:00:24,939 --> 00:00:26,139
So five chicks.

9
00:00:26,329 --> 00:00:27,739
Every egg hatches!

10
00:00:27,867 --> 00:00:28,737
Round one.

11
00:00:28,997 --> 00:00:30,157
Is Momo right?

12
00:00:30,357 --> 00:00:31,757
Does every egg hatch?

13
00:00:32,007 --> 00:00:33,067
One, yes.

14
00:00:33,287 --> 00:00:34,087
Two, no.

15
00:00:34,327 --> 00:00:35,737
Show me with your fingers.

16
00:00:41,203 --> 00:00:42,153
Locked in!

17
00:00:42,283 --> 00:00:42,843
One.

18
00:00:42,593 --> 00:00:45,263
The hen kept all five warm.

19
00:00:45,318 --> 00:00:45,958
Two!

20
00:00:46,088 --> 00:00:47,498
Lizards lay eggs too.

21
00:00:47,588 --> 00:00:49,678
Grandma says not every egg hatches.

22
00:00:50,250 --> 00:00:51,010
Watch.

23
00:00:52,460 --> 00:00:53,310
A crack.

24
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:54,450
A beak.

25
00:00:54,930 --> 00:00:55,790
One chick!

26
00:00:56,240 --> 00:00:56,890
Two.

27
00:00:57,300 --> 00:00:58,060
Three.

28
00:00:58,460 --> 00:00:59,200
Four!

29
00:01:00,612 --> 00:01:02,152
One egg did not hatch.

30
00:01:02,372 --> 00:01:03,662
The answer is two.

31
00:01:03,902 --> 00:01:05,462
Not every egg hatches.

32
00:01:05,970 --> 00:01:06,420
Oh.

33
00:01:06,170 --> 00:01:07,920
Only four.

34
00:01:08,008 --> 00:01:09,318
That happens, Momo.

35
00:01:09,588 --> 00:01:11,858
It is why some animals lay many eggs.

36
00:01:11,993 --> 00:01:12,983
Two fingers?

37
00:01:13,213 --> 00:01:14,373
One finger up!

38
00:01:14,797 --> 00:01:16,227
Yesterday, one hen.

39
00:01:16,447 --> 00:01:18,597
Today, one hen and four chicks.

40
00:01:19,179 --> 00:01:20,379
Say it with me!

41
00:01:21,091 --> 00:01:23,941
When living things reproduce, there will be more of them.

42
00:01:24,141 --> 00:01:28,621
The ability to reproduce is important to ensure the survival of all

43
00:01:28,371 --> 00:01:29,411
living things.

44
00:01:33,451 --> 00:01:37,121
Some animals reproduce by giving birth to their young alive.

45
00:01:37,169 --> 00:01:38,089
Kittens!

46
00:01:38,189 --> 00:01:39,829
I was born like that too.

47
00:01:40,035 --> 00:01:43,585
Other animals lay eggs and their young hatch out of the eggs.

48
00:01:44,018 --> 00:01:45,678
But plants cannot lay eggs!

49
00:01:45,802 --> 00:01:48,442
Some plants reproduce by producing seeds.

50
00:01:48,541 --> 00:01:49,871
Like this tomato.

51
00:01:50,447 --> 00:01:52,867
Other plants can reproduce through spores.

52
00:01:52,969 --> 00:01:54,069
Like this fern.

53
00:01:54,309 --> 00:01:55,569
Look under the leaf.

54
00:01:58,094 --> 00:01:59,854
Why does reproducing matter?

55
00:02:00,064 --> 00:02:03,553
Without new young, a kind of living thing would disappear forever.

56
00:02:03,784 --> 00:02:05,553
Scientists call that extinct.

57
00:02:11,011 --> 00:02:12,240
And the four chicks?

58
00:02:12,471 --> 00:02:13,691
Now they will grow.

59
00:02:13,741 --> 00:02:15,431
So a chick just gets bigger?

60
00:02:15,531 --> 00:02:16,801
A giant chick?

61
00:02:16,928 --> 00:02:18,208
Not a giant chick.

62
00:02:18,458 --> 00:02:19,448
Look at the hen.

63
00:02:19,708 --> 00:02:20,818
Bigger, yes.

64
00:02:21,018 --> 00:02:24,388
But she also has wing feathers, a red comb and a tail.

65
00:02:24,902 --> 00:02:26,112
Say it with me!

66
00:02:26,827 --> 00:02:29,607
When living things grow, they increase in size.

67
00:02:29,817 --> 00:02:31,867
They also become more complex.

68
00:02:35,899 --> 00:02:37,279
You grow like that too.

69
00:02:37,529 --> 00:02:38,249
Bigger.

70
00:02:38,489 --> 00:02:42,519
And more complex: more hair, longer arms and legs, new teeth.

71
00:02:42,951 --> 00:02:47,791
Like animals, plants also increase in size and become more complex as

72
00:02:47,541 --> 00:02:48,321
they grow.

73
00:02:48,452 --> 00:02:49,302
A seed.

74
00:02:49,522 --> 00:02:50,602
A small plant.

75
00:02:50,842 --> 00:02:53,432
A bigger plant, with more leaves and flowers.

76
00:02:56,469 --> 00:02:57,679
My trail notes!

77
00:02:57,804 --> 00:02:58,364
One.

78
00:02:58,504 --> 00:03:00,514
A hen reproduces by laying eggs.

79
00:03:00,534 --> 00:03:01,234
Two.

80
00:03:01,324 --> 00:03:03,604
A tomato plant, by producing seeds.

81
00:03:03,664 --> 00:03:04,404
Three.

82
00:03:04,474 --> 00:03:06,234
A fern, by producing seeds.

83
00:03:06,586 --> 00:03:07,466
Showdown!

84
00:03:07,706 --> 00:03:08,796
Double points.

85
00:03:09,036 --> 00:03:10,616
One line has a mistake.

86
00:03:10,846 --> 00:03:12,556
Line one, two, or three?

87
00:03:12,786 --> 00:03:14,196
Show me with your fingers.

88
00:03:20,664 --> 00:03:21,614
Locked in!

89
00:03:21,744 --> 00:03:22,564
Three.

90
00:03:22,764 --> 00:03:24,824
Those brown dots are spores.

91
00:03:24,870 --> 00:03:25,700
Three!

92
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:27,300
Spores, not seeds.

93
00:03:27,351 --> 00:03:27,981
Oops!

94
00:03:28,859 --> 00:03:29,399
Fixed!

95
00:03:29,149 --> 00:03:31,609
A fern reproduces through spores.

96
00:03:31,739 --> 00:03:33,069
Showed three fingers?

97
00:03:33,259 --> 00:03:35,159
Put up two more on your score hand!

98
00:03:35,660 --> 00:03:36,700
Three stamps!

99
00:03:36,940 --> 00:03:38,180
Grow: the chicks.

100
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:39,970
Respond: the mimosa.

101
00:03:40,260 --> 00:03:41,740
Reproduce: the hen.

102
00:03:42,326 --> 00:03:43,526
Say it with me!

103
00:03:44,238 --> 00:03:47,678
Living things grow, respond to changes and reproduce.

104
00:03:50,052 --> 00:03:51,115
Say it with us!

105
00:03:51,774 --> 00:03:52,424
Grow!

106
00:03:52,186 --> 00:03:52,857
Respond!

107
00:03:52,599 --> 00:03:53,629
Reproduce!

108
00:03:55,074 --> 00:03:56,824
That's what living things can DO!

109
00:03:58,374 --> 00:03:59,024
Grow!

110
00:03:58,786 --> 00:03:59,456
Respond!

111
00:03:59,199 --> 00:04:00,229
Reproduce!

112
00:04:00,024 --> 00:04:01,774
That's what living things can DO!

113
00:04:02,629 --> 00:04:04,019
Show me your score hand!

114
00:04:04,249 --> 00:04:05,259
More than two?

115
00:04:05,489 --> 00:04:06,769
You beat the Buddies.

116
00:04:06,989 --> 00:04:08,049
Three fingers?

117
00:04:08,269 --> 00:04:09,269
High five!

118
00:04:09,766 --> 00:04:11,126
Did you spot the lizard?

119
00:04:11,376 --> 00:04:12,976
It was on the hen house wall.

120
00:04:13,166 --> 00:04:16,406
Does a lizard lay eggs, or give birth to its young alive?

121
00:04:16,636 --> 00:04:17,916
Tell someone at home.

122
00:04:19,302 --> 00:04:20,062
A hen.

123
00:04:20,282 --> 00:04:21,102
A cat.

124
00:04:21,332 --> 00:04:22,602
A tomato plant.

125
00:04:22,812 --> 00:04:23,662
A fern.

126
00:04:23,882 --> 00:04:26,132
There are millions of kinds of living things!

127
00:04:26,332 --> 00:04:29,062
But scientists sort them into only four groups.

128
00:04:29,272 --> 00:04:30,612
Can you guess the four?

129
00:04:30,842 --> 00:04:32,712
Lock in your answer for next time.

130
00:04:33,449 --> 00:04:35,609
Let's read today's key words together!

131
00:04:35,849 --> 00:04:36,569
Ready?

132
00:04:36,859 --> 00:04:37,749
Reproduce.

133
00:04:39,299 --> 00:04:40,139
Spores.

134
00:04:41,575 --> 00:04:42,319
Extinct.

135
00:04:43,830 --> 00:04:45,050
More complex.

136
00:04:46,689 --> 00:04:47,589
Well done!

137
00:04:48,572 --> 00:04:49,628
Say it with us!

138
00:04:50,428 --> 00:04:51,138
Ready?

139
00:04:51,262 --> 00:04:51,882
One!

140
00:04:52,262 --> 00:04:52,942
two!

141
00:04:53,267 --> 00:04:54,442
Now you know!
